# Agents : stockés dans db_bank. La connexion est un POST JSON, sans formulaire Keycloak.
# 1. docker compose up -d db_bank
# 2. mvn spring-boot:run
# 3. Exécuter « Jeton admin1 » ci-dessous (Body raw JSON dans Postman).
# 4. Créer un agent, puis bloquer un compte avec ce jeton (docs/bank-api.http).
# Le jeton wallet reste obtenu auprès de Keycloak, en x-www-form-urlencoded.

@api = http://localhost:9081
@auth = http://localhost:8180

### Jeton admin1
POST {{api}}/bank/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "admin1",
  "password": "admin123"
}

> {%
    client.global.set("adminToken", response.body.access_token);
%}

### Jeton auditor1
POST {{api}}/bank/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "auditor1",
  "password": "auditor123"
}

> {%
    client.global.set("auditorToken", response.body.access_token);
%}

### Créer un agent
POST {{api}}/bank/admin/agents
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "agent2",
  "password": "agent12345",
  "firstName": "Claire",
  "lastName": "Agent",
  "email": "agent2@bank.local",
  "role": "BANK_ADMIN"
}

### Lister les agents
GET {{api}}/bank/admin/agents
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

### Jeton wallet
POST {{auth}}/realms/bank/protocol/openid-connect/token
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=client_credentials&client_id=wallet-service&client_secret=wallet-dev-secret

> {%
    client.global.set("walletToken", response.body.access_token);
%}
